<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Western Wei (206 BC)</h1><p class="st-meta">206 BCE – 205 BCE · capital Pingyang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Wei Bao's kingdom in southern Shanxi, one of the Eighteen Kingdoms of 206 BCE, conquered by Han Xin's surprise crossing of the Yellow River.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-bao">Wei Bao</a>, of the royal house of the old <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-wei">State of Wei</a>, had been made King of Wei during the rising against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu's</a> settlement of 206 BCE he lost the Wei heartland around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Daliang</a> and was moved west to rule as King of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-wei">Western Wei</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pingyang-shanxi">Pingyang</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fen-river">Fen</a> valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom covered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedong-shanxi">Hedong</a> region, rich in salt and grain, on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a> region. It was a frontline state whose loyalty swung with the fortunes of Chu and Han.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-bao">Wei Bao</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> in 205 BCE, then deserted him after the Han defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuzhou">Pengcheng</a>. That autumn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-xin">Han Xin</a> feinted at one ford while crossing at Xiayang on rafts of wooden jars, and captured Wei Bao. His concubine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-bo">Bo Ji</a> later bore Liu Bang a son, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-han">Emperor Wen of Han</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/western-wei-206-bc.jpg" alt="Western Wei (206 BC)" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_Wei_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>206 BCE – 205 BCE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pingyang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wei Bao</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Western Wei (Wei Bao)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-206&amp;focus=western-wei-206-bc" aria-label="Western Wei (206 BC) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7449 935 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Western Wei (206 BC) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7769 1191L7739 1235"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7739 1235h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7769 1191h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=-206&amp;focus=western-wei-206-bc">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

